5. WORLD MAP
==================================================

The world map is a stage graph rather than simply a linear list.

The player begins at the bottom-left.

Only the first stage is initially unlocked.

Stages can have:

- one next stage
- multiple branches
- locked paths
- progression requirements
- optional stages
- miniboss stages
- major boss stages
- shops
- NPC locations
- special locations

Example progression:

              NORTH
                ↑
          [Lava Mountains]
                ↑
           [Final Boss]
                ↑
       ┌────────┴────────┐
       │                 │
    [Stage]           [Stage]
       │                 │
       └───────┬─────────┘
               │
            [Stage]
               │
          [Starting Area]
               │
          [Player Hut]

Do NOT hardcode map progression into individual stage classes.

Represent the map as data.

Each MapNode should support:

- id
- name
- position
- stageId
- type
- unlocked
- completed
- prerequisites
- connections
- rewards
- specialRequirements

==================================================
6. PROGRESSION GATES
==================================================

Support world obstacles that require completing other stages or obtaining special items.

Examples:

BROKEN BRIDGE:

The player cannot travel north.

Requirement:

Defeat an eastern miniboss.

Reward:

Bridge Logs.

Once the player obtains Bridge Logs:

The northern bridge becomes available.

Another example:

LOCKED GATE:

The western gate is closed.

Requirement:

Defeat a southern boss.

Reward:

Ancient Key.

The key unlocks the western path.

Create a generic:

ProgressionRequirement

system.

It should support future requirements such as:

- kill boss
- defeat miniboss
- obtain item
- unlock spell
- unlock pet
- reach level
- complete stage
- complete multiple stages
- possess enough gold
- possess specific rare item

Do not hardcode only the bridge/key examples.

==================================================
7. STAGE ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Stages must be modular and support different gameplay styles.

Create a base Stage class/interface.

Each stage should have:

- id
- name
- description
- type
- difficulty
- waves
- enemies
- rewards
- minibosses
- boss
- level
- player rules
- environment
- completion conditions

The StageManager should load stages through a StageFactory.

Do NOT use large if/else blocks such as:

if(type === "open") ...
else if(type === "tower") ...
else if(type === "future") ...

Use a registry/factory/plugin-style architecture so new stage types can be added without modifying the core StageManager.

==================================================
8. INITIAL STAGE TYPES
==================================================

Implement architecture for at least:

OPEN_FIELD

Player can freely move around the stage.

Enemies spawn around the playable area.

Enemies approach and attack the player.

DEFENSE

Player is stationary.

Enemies spawn from different directions and move toward the player.

This should behave somewhat like tower defense.

Create the base interfaces necessary for future stage types such as:

- escort
- survival
- boss arena
- timed challenge
- puzzle
- dungeon
- moving stage
- objective-based stage

These do not need to be implemented now.

==================================================
9. STAGE LEVEL SYSTEM
==================================================

Every stage has its own temporary level.

When entering a stage:

level = 0
experience = 0

The level resets when entering a new stage.

Levels do NOT carry between stages.

Enemies award EXP.

When enough EXP is collected:

level increases.

Do not confuse stage level with permanent progression.

The permanent progression system must remain separate.

==================================================
10. LEVEL-UP CARD SYSTEM
==================================================

Whenever the player levels up:

Pause gameplay.

Generate 3 random cards.

The player must select exactly one.

Cards can provide:

- stat upgrades
- new spells
- spell modifications
- elemental bonuses
- health bonuses
- movement bonuses
- attack bonuses
- attack speed
- cooldown reduction
- lifesteal
- regeneration
- temporary stage-specific effects

The card system must be data-driven.

Create a Card interface/data model:

{
    id,
    name,
    description,
    rarity,
    type,
    effect,
    prerequisites
}

Do not hardcode card choices into the UI.

==================================================
11. SPELL SYSTEM
==================================================

At the beginning of every stage:

The player is presented with 3 available spell cards.

The player chooses 1.

The selected spell becomes part of the player's current stage spell loadout.

Spells automatically fire when a valid enemy enters their targeting range.

The player does not manually cast the basic spells.

The architecture must support:

- single-target spells
- area-of-effect spells
- projectiles
- instant damage
- damage-over-time
- chain attacks
- piercing
- buffs
- debuffs
- future spell mechanics

Every spell should have configurable:

- base damage
- cooldown
- range
- targeting mode
- projectile speed
- area radius
- elemental damage
- elemental type
- target count

==================================================
12. ELEMENTAL SYSTEM
==================================================

Initial elements:

- FIRE
- THUNDER
- WIND

Design the ElementSystem so additional elements can be added later without rewriting combat logic.

Spells can have:

baseDamage

+

elementalDamage

Example:

Fireball:

base damage = 20
fire damage = 15

Total damage before defenses:

35

Do not hardcode elemental logic inside individual spells.

Use a centralized ElementSystem.

==================================================
13. ELEMENTAL DEFENSE
==================================================

Enemies have:

- health
- damage
- defense
- elemental resistances
- elemental weaknesses

Enemies can resist elements but normal enemies should never be completely immune.

Bosses and minibosses may have elemental immunity.

The architecture must distinguish:

RESISTANCE

from

IMMUNITY

Example:

Fire resistance = 40%

Fire damage:

finalFireDamage =
baseFireDamage × (1 - 0.40)

For immunity:

finalFireDamage = 0

Make this configurable per enemy.

Support:

- physical defense
- fire resistance
- thunder resistance
- wind resistance
- future elements

Do not hardcode element-specific calculations into the Enemy class.

