16. GOLD SYSTEM
==================================================

Introduce Gold as a run-independent currency.

Enemies have a configurable chance to drop Gold.

Example:

Skeleton:
10% chance

Goblin:
15% chance

Bat:
12% chance

Elite:
30% chance

Boss:
100% chance

Gold is used for:

- unlocking spells
- upgrading spells
- permanent upgrades

Gold should persist using localStorage.

Keep Gold separate from:

- EXP
- stage level
- Souls
- future boss items

Use a centralized Currency/Economy system so more currencies can be added later.

==================================================
17. PERMANENT UPGRADE TREE
==================================================

Create a large permanent upgrade tree using Gold.

The tree should be data-driven.

When the player purchases an upgrade:

- Gold is deducted.
- The node becomes unlocked.
- New dependent nodes become available.
- The upgrade persists between runs.
- The effect is applied to future runs.

Initial upgrade categories should support:

- maximum health
- gold drop chance
- spell slots
- global spell cooldown reduction
- elemental upgrades
- pet slot unlock
- additional pet slots
- faster EXP/level progression
- movement speed
- attack damage
- attack speed
- lifesteal
- regeneration
- defense
- spell combo unlocks

Do NOT assume spell combos are finalized.

Design the upgrade architecture so "spell combos" can be added later without rewriting the upgrade system.

The UI should visually resemble a large branching skill tree.

A node can unlock additional child nodes.

==================================================
18. SPELL SHOP
==================================================

After completing the first 3 stages:

Unlock the Spell Shop.

The shop allows the player to:

- purchase new spells
- upgrade existing spells

Initially:

Gold is the only required currency.

However, design the system for future requirements.

Future spell unlock requirements may include:

- Gold
- miniboss-specific item
- major boss-specific item
- rare enemy drop
- special quest item

Future spell upgrade requirements may use the same system.

Therefore, DO NOT implement:

cost = number

as the only possible cost representation.

Instead support something like:

costs: [
    { type: "gold", amount: 100 },
    { type: "item", itemId: "boss_crystal", amount: 1 }
]

This allows additional resource types later.

Spells available inside stages must ONLY be spells that the player has unlocked.

==================================================
19. SPELL SLOTS
==================================================

The player has a limited number of spell slots.

The permanent upgrade tree can increase the number of spell slots.

The spell system must distinguish between:

UNLOCKED SPELLS

and

EQUIPPED SPELLS

and

AVAILABLE STAGE SPELLS

A player may own many spells but only equip/use a limited number depending on progression.

Keep these concepts separate.

==================================================
20. PET SYSTEM
==================================================

After several stages, introduce an NPC:

Animal Caretaker.

The caretaker unlocks the Pet system.

Pets have elemental affiliations.

Initial examples:

Fire Pet
Thunder Pet
Wind Pet

A pet increases elemental damage of the corresponding element.

Example:

Fire Pet:

+15% Fire Damage

Pets should be data-driven and support future effects beyond elemental bonuses.

The permanent upgrade tree should be able to:

- unlock the first pet slot
- increase the number of pet slots

Do not hardcode a maximum number of pet slots.

==================================================
21. STAGE DIFFICULTY
==================================================

The game is intentionally difficult.

The player should expect to:

- die
- retry stages
- experiment with builds
- grind gold
- unlock permanent upgrades
- discover effective spell combinations
- learn enemy weaknesses

However, difficulty scaling should be data-driven.

Support:

- enemy HP scaling
- enemy damage scaling
- spawn rate
- enemy count
- enemy variety
- wave count
- minibosses
- bosses

Do not hardcode difficulty scaling inside individual enemies.

==================================================
22. RUN VS PERMANENT PROGRESSION
==================================================

Clearly separate temporary stage progression from permanent progression.

TEMPORARY:

- current stage level
- current EXP
- selected level-up cards
- current stage spells
- temporary spell modifications
- temporary buffs
- current enemies
- current stage state

PERSISTENT:

- unlocked stages
- completed stages
- Gold
- unlocked spells
- spell upgrades
- permanent upgrade tree
- unlocked pets
- pet slots
- progression items
- boss items
- rare items
- story progression

The SaveManager should be responsible for persistent data.

